<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-differential-equations">
    <title>Differential Equations</title>

    <section xml:id="sec-intro-de">
        <title>Introduction to Differential Equations</title>

        <p>
            A differential equation is an equation that relates a function
            to one or more of its derivatives.
        </p>

        <p>
            For example,
        </p>

        <me>
            \frac{dy}{dt}=3y
        </me>

        <p>
            is a differential equation relating the unknown function
            <m>y(t)</m> to its derivative.
        </p>

        <definition xml:id="def-solution-de">
            <title>Solution of a Differential Equation</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    To solve a differential equation means to find a function
                    that satisfies the equation.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            We will consider three important types of first-order differential
            equations:
        </p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    pure-time differential equations,
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    autonomous differential equations,
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    general separable differential equations.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-pure-time-de">
        <title>Pure-Time Differential Equations</title>

        <p>
            A pure-time differential equation has the form
        </p>

        <me>
            \frac{dy}{dt}=f(t),
        </me>

        <p>
            where the right-hand side depends only on time.
        </p>

        <p>
            These equations can be solved directly by integration:
        </p>

        <me>
            y(t)=\int f(t)\,dt.
        </me>

        <example xml:id="ex-pure-time-cell-volume">
            <title>Cell volume</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose the volume <m>V(t)</m> of a cell changes according
                    to
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dV}{dt}=\sin t,
                    \qquad
                    V(0)=3.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Find <m>V(t)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Integrating,
                </p>

                <me>
                    V(t)=-\cos t+C.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Apply the initial condition:
                </p>

                <me>
                    3=V(0)=-\cos 0+C=-1+C.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Hence <m>C=4</m>, so
                </p>

                <me>
                    V(t)=4-\cos t.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-autonomous-de">
        <title>Autonomous Differential Equations</title>

        <p>
            An autonomous differential equation has the form
        </p>

        <me>
            \frac{dy}{dt}=g(y),
        </me>

        <p>
            where the rate of change depends only on the current state
            <m>y</m>, not explicitly on time.
        </p>

        <p>
            If the equation can be rearranged so that all terms involving
            <m>y</m> are on one side and all terms involving <m>t</m> are on
            the other, we can solve it by separation of variables.
        </p>

        <me>
            \frac{1}{g(y)}\,dy=dt.
        </me>

        <p>
            Integrating both sides gives
        </p>

        <me>
            \int\frac{1}{g(y)}\,dy
            =
            \int dt.
        </me>

        <example xml:id="ex-exponential-growth">
            <title>Exponential growth and decay</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose a population <m>N(t)</m> satisfies
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dN}{dt}=rN,
                    \qquad
                    N(0)=100.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Find <m>N(t)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Separate variables:
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{1}{N}\,dN=r\,dt.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Integrating,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \ln|N|=rt+C.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Exponentiating,
                </p>

                <me>
                    |N|=e^{rt+C}=e^Ce^{rt}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Absorb the sign and the factor <m>e^C</m> into a single
                    constant <m>A</m>:
                </p>

                <me>
                    N(t)=Ae^{rt}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Using <m>N(0)=100</m>,
                </p>

                <me>
                    A=100.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    N(t)=100e^{rt}.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <remark xml:id="rem-exp-growth-decay">
            <title>Growth and decay</title>

            <p>
                In the model
                <m>N(t)=N_0e^{rt}</m>:
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        if <m>r&gt;0</m>, the population grows exponentially;
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        if <m>r=0</m>, the population remains constant;
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        if <m>r&lt;0</m>, the population decays exponentially.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </remark>

        <example xml:id="ex-von-bertalanffy">
            <title>Von Bertalanffy growth model</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose the length <m>L(t)</m> of a fish satisfies
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dL}{dt}
                    =
                    k(34-L),
                    \qquad
                    L(0)=2,
                </me>

                <p>
                    where <m>k&gt;0</m>. Find <m>L(t)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Separate variables:
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{1}{34-L}\,dL
                    =
                    k\,dt.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Integrating,
                </p>

                <me>
                    -\ln|34-L|
                    =
                    kt+C.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Rearranging and exponentiating gives
                </p>

                <me>
                    |34-L|
                    =
                    Ae^{-kt}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Absorbing the sign into the constant,
                </p>

                <me>
                    L=34-Ae^{-kt}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Apply the initial condition:
                </p>

                <me>
                    2=34-A,
                </me>

                <p>
                    so <m>A=32</m>. Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    L(t)=34-32e^{-kt}.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-separable-de">
        <title>Separable Differential Equations</title>

        <p>
            A general separable differential equation has the form
        </p>

        <me>
            \frac{dy}{dt}=f(t)g(y).
        </me>

        <p>
            The right-hand side is a product of a function of time and a
            function of the state variable.
        </p>

        <p>
            Rearranging,
        </p>

        <me>
            \frac{1}{g(y)}\,dy
            =
            f(t)\,dt.
        </me>

        <p>
            We then integrate both sides.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-general-separable">
            <title>A general separable equation</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dy}{dx}
                    =
                    \frac{xy}{x+1},
                    \qquad
                    y(0)=1.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Separate variables:
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{1}{y}\,dy
                    =
                    \frac{x}{x+1}\,dx.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Rewrite
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{x}{x+1}
                    =
                    1-\frac{1}{x+1}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Integrating,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \ln|y|
                    =
                    x-\ln|x+1|+C.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Exponentiating,
                </p>

                <me>
                    |y|
                    =
                    e^x e^{-\ln|x+1|}e^C.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Absorbing constants and signs into a constant <m>A</m>,
                </p>

                <me>
                    y
                    =
                    A\frac{e^x}{x+1}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Since <m>y(0)=1</m>,
                </p>

                <me>
                    A=1.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    y(x)=\frac{e^x}{x+1}.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <example xml:id="ex-periodic-cell-growth">
            <title>Cell division with a circadian rhythm</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose the number of cells <m>N(t)</m> satisfies
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dN}{dt}
                    =
                    \frac{1}{2}
                    \left(
                    1+\sin(2\pi t)
                    \right)N,
                    \qquad
                    N(0)=N_0.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Find <m>N(t)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Separate variables:
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{1}{N}\,dN
                    =
                    \frac{1}{2}
                    \left(
                    1+\sin(2\pi t)
                    \right)\,dt.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Integrating,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \ln|N|
                    =
                    \frac{t}{2}
                    -
                    \frac{1}{4\pi}\cos(2\pi t)
                    +
                    C.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    N(t)
                    =
                    A
                    \exp
                    \left(
                    \frac{t}{2}
                    -
                    \frac{1}{4\pi}\cos(2\pi t)
                    \right).
                </me>

                <p>
                    Applying <m>N(0)=N_0</m> gives
                </p>

                <me>
                    A
                    =
                    N_0e^{1/(4\pi)}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Hence,
                </p>

                <me>
                    N(t)
                    =
                    N_0
                    \exp
                    \left(
                    \frac{t}{2}
                    +
                    \frac{1}{4\pi}
                    \left(
                    1-\cos(2\pi t)
                    \right)
                    \right).
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-equilibria-stability">
        <title>Equilibria and Stability</title>

        <p>
            For an autonomous differential equation
        </p>

        <me>
            \frac{dy}{dt}=f(y),
        </me>

        <p>
            an equilibrium is a state at which the solution does not change
            with time.
        </p>

        <definition xml:id="def-equilibrium">
            <title>Equilibrium</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    A value <m>y^*</m> is an equilibrium if
                </p>

                <me>
                    f(y^*)=0.
                </me>

                <p>
                    The corresponding solution is constant:
                </p>

                <me>
                    y(t)=y^*.
                </me>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <example xml:id="ex-find-equilibria">
            <title>Finding equilibria</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find all equilibria of
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dy}{dt}
                    =
                    y^3-3y^2+2y.
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Set the right-hand side equal to zero:
                </p>

                <me>
                    y^3-3y^2+2y=0.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Factor:
                </p>

                <me>
                    y(y-1)(y-2)=0.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore, the equilibria are
                </p>

                <me>
                    y^*=0,
                    \qquad
                    y^*=1,
                    \qquad
                    y^*=2.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <definition xml:id="def-stability">
            <title>Stable and Unstable Equilibria</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    An equilibrium is <term>stable</term> if nearby solutions
                    move toward it.
                </p>

                <p>
                    An equilibrium is <term>unstable</term> if nearby solutions
                    move away from it.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p>
            Stability can often be determined without solving the differential
            equation. We examine the sign of <m>f(y)</m>.
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    If <m>f(y)&gt;0</m>, then <m>dy/dt&gt;0</m>, so
                    <m>y(t)</m> increases.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    If <m>f(y)&lt;0</m>, then <m>dy/dt&lt;0</m>, so
                    <m>y(t)</m> decreases.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>

        <example xml:id="ex-logistic-stability">
            <title>Stability in a bacterial population model</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dN}{dt}=N(5-N).
                </me>

                <p>
                    Find the equilibria and determine their stability.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The equilibria satisfy
                </p>

                <me>
                    N(5-N)=0.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Hence,
                </p>

                <me>
                    N^*=0
                    \qquad\text{or}\qquad
                    N^*=5.
                </me>

                <p>
                    For <m>0&lt;N&lt;5</m>,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dN}{dt}&gt;0,
                </me>

                <p>
                    so solutions move to the right, toward <m>N=5</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    For <m>N&gt;5</m>,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dN}{dt}&lt;0,
                </me>

                <p>
                    so solutions move to the left, again toward <m>N=5</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Thus, <m>N=5</m> is stable.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Near <m>N=0</m>, solutions move away from zero, so
                    <m>N=0</m> is unstable.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

        <remark xml:id="rem-carrying-capacity">
            <title>Carrying capacity</title>

            <p>
                In a population model, a stable positive equilibrium is often
                interpreted as the carrying capacity of the environment.
            </p>
        </remark>

        <example xml:id="ex-allee-effect">
            <title>A model with an Allee threshold</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dN}{dt}
                    =
                    N(N-1)
                    \left(
                    1-\frac{N}{3}
                    \right).
                </me>

                <p>
                    Find the equilibria and determine their stability.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The equilibria satisfy
                </p>

                <me>
                    N(N-1)
                    \left(
                    1-\frac{N}{3}
                    \right)
                    =
                    0.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    N^*=0,
                    \qquad
                    N^*=1,
                    \qquad
                    N^*=3.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Examining the sign of the right-hand side on the intervals
                    separated by these equilibria shows that
                </p>

                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>N=0</m> is stable,</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>N=1</m> is unstable,</p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p><m>N=3</m> is stable.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>

                <p>
                    The unstable equilibrium <m>N=1</m> acts as a threshold:
                    populations below it tend toward extinction, while
                    populations above it tend toward the positive stable
                    equilibrium.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-eigenvalue-method">
        <title>The Derivative Test for Stability</title>

        <p>
            There is also a non-graphical way to determine the stability of
            an equilibrium.
        </p>

        <p>
            Suppose
        </p>

        <me>
            \frac{dy}{dt}=f(y)
        </me>

        <p>
            and <m>y^*</m> is an equilibrium, so <m>f(y^*)=0</m>.
        </p>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-stability-derivative">
            <title>Derivative Test for Stability</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>y^*</m> be an equilibrium of
                    <m>dy/dt=f(y)</m>.
                </p>

                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If <m>f'(y^*)&lt;0</m>, then
                            <m>y^*</m> is stable.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If <m>f'(y^*)&gt;0</m>, then
                            <m>y^*</m> is unstable.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            If <m>f'(y^*)=0</m>, the test is inconclusive.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <remark xml:id="rem-eigenvalue-name">
            <title>Eigenvalue</title>

            <p>
                In this one-dimensional setting, the quantity
                <m>f'(y^*)</m> is often referred to as the eigenvalue of the
                equilibrium.
            </p>
        </remark>

        <example xml:id="ex-eigenvalue-logistic">
            <title>Using the derivative test</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine the stability of the equilibria of
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dN}{dt}
                    =
                    N(5-N).
                </me>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Let
                </p>

                <me>
                    f(N)=N(5-N)=5N-N^2.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Then
                </p>

                <me>
                    f'(N)=5-2N.
                </me>

                <p>
                    At <m>N=0</m>,
                </p>

                <me>
                    f'(0)=5&gt;0,
                </me>

                <p>
                    so <m>N=0</m> is unstable.
                </p>

                <p>
                    At <m>N=5</m>,
                </p>

                <me>
                    f'(5)=-5&lt;0,
                </me>

                <p>
                    so <m>N=5</m> is stable.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-long-term-behavior">
        <title>Long-Term Behavior</title>

        <p>
            Once the equilibria and their stability are known, we can often
            determine the long-term behavior of a solution without explicitly
            solving the differential equation.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-long-term-logistic">
            <title>Long-term behavior from stability</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dN}{dt}=N(5-N).
                </me>

                <p>
                    Determine the long-term behavior for several initial
                    conditions.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The equilibrium <m>N=5</m> is stable, while
                    <m>N=0</m> is unstable.
                </p>

                <p>
                    If
                </p>

                <me>
                    0&lt;N(0)&lt;5,
                </me>

                <p>
                    then the solution increases toward <m>5</m>, so
                </p>

                <me>
                    \lim_{t\to\infty}N(t)=5.
                </me>

                <p>
                    If
                </p>

                <me>
                    N(0)&gt;5,
                </me>

                <p>
                    then the solution decreases toward <m>5</m>, so again
                </p>

                <me>
                    \lim_{t\to\infty}N(t)=5.
                </me>

                <p>
                    If a mathematically possible initial condition satisfies
                    <m>N(0)&lt;0</m>, the vector field points away from zero
                    toward more negative values, so the solution does not
                    approach the positive equilibrium.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-de-models">
        <title>Applications of Differential Equations</title>

        <p>
            Differential equations provide models for many biological
            processes. The main task is often to translate a verbal
            description of rates into an equation and then interpret the
            resulting solution.
        </p>

        <example xml:id="ex-fish-restocking">
            <title>A fish population with restocking and mortality</title>

            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>P(t)</m> be the fish population in a pond.
                    Suppose fish are added at a constant rate <m>r</m> and
                    die at a rate proportional to the current population,
                    with constant <m>k&gt;0</m>. Then
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{dP}{dt}
                    =
                    r-kP,
                    \qquad
                    P(0)=0.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Find <m>P(t)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Separate variables:
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{1}{r-kP}\,dP
                    =
                    dt.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Integrating,
                </p>

                <me>
                    -\frac{1}{k}\ln|r-kP|
                    =
                    t+C.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Rearranging,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \ln|r-kP|
                    =
                    -kt+C_1.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Exponentiating and absorbing constants,
                </p>

                <me>
                    r-kP
                    =
                    Ae^{-kt}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Thus,
                </p>

                <me>
                    P(t)
                    =
                    \frac{1}{k}
                    \left(
                    r-Ae^{-kt}
                    \right).
                </me>

                <p>
                    Apply <m>P(0)=0</m>:
                </p>

                <me>
                    0
                    =
                    \frac{1}{k}(r-A),
                </me>

                <p>
                    so <m>A=r</m>. Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    P(t)
                    =
                    \frac{r}{k}
                    \left(
                    1-e^{-kt}
                    \right).
                </me>
            </solution>
        </example>

    </section>

</chapter>